\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
The increasing deployment of large language models (LLMs) in creative tasks raises critical questions regarding their limitations and capabilities. This paper investigates the creative boundaries of LLMs through enhanced strategies like multi-token prediction (MTP) and seed-conditioning. Traditional next-token prediction methods often yield limited outputs, constraining the models to a narrow creative scope. In contrast, MTP enables a broader exploration of possible outputs by predicting multiple tokens simultaneously, which can lead to more innovative and diverse results. The introduction of seed-conditioning, which injects randomness into the input layer, further aims to enhance creativity without compromising output coherence. Our findings suggest that these methods can provide a significant advantage in tasks requiring abstract reasoning and planning.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
The exploration of creative limits in LLMs has been sparsely addressed in prior literature. Previous studies, such as those by Kalinsky et al. (2023) and Walker (2024), highlight advancements in multi-token completion and causal language modeling. However, these works do not explicitly assess the creative potential of LLMs in open-ended tasks. Additionally, Zhu et al. (2023) and Hassan et al. (2024) discuss methods that introduce randomness during model training and output generation, which resonate with our seed-conditioning approach. Unlike previous studies, our work establishes a novel framework for assessing creativity through a tailored test bed of algorithmic tasks, addressing both the theoretical and practical implications of these techniques.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
We designed a set of minimal algorithmic tasks that require creative leaps, such as generating analogies, solving complex problems, or designing mathematical challenges. Our approach compares outputs from LLMs trained using traditional next-token prediction against those utilizing MTP and seed-conditioning. The seed-conditioning technique involves adding random noise to the input data, allowing for a more diverse exploration of output space. This method is evaluated alongside conventional temperature sampling methods to assess its effectiveness in enhancing creativity.

